{{!-- CLASSIC layout only. The Homefront Moves sidebar: a permanent right-hand column,
      visible from every tab, with a collapse handle whose state is remembered per actor per
      user (the `characterSidebarCollapsed` map, via get/setSidebarCollapsed - the key name
      predates the steading using it, and renaming it would drop everyone's stored state).
      In the MODERN layout this whole panel is the `moves` TAB instead; see
      steading-tab-moves.hbs.

      The move ROWS are the one part of the classic/modern split that is not a re-mount.
      Modern renders move CARDS that show their text outright and take the bordered box from
      the shared `.tab.moves .stonetop-item` rules; classic renders one-line rows in a narrow
      column whose text is carried in a hidden div and shown by the hover panel wired in
      StonetopSteadingSheet#activateListeners. Different wrapper, different item element,
      different description visibility - two shapes, deliberately, in two files.

      This file must stay separate from steading-tab-moves.hbs rather than branching inside
      it: that file is pinned by tests/actors/steading/roll-prompt.test.js, which requires it
      to carry no roll-mode control of any kind and exactly one move group.

      There is no Roll Modifier section here any more, in either layout. It used to head this
      column as a stacked radio list (and the modern tab as a segmented pill), and it stayed
      set: a player who took Advantage for one roll kept it for every roll after, which is
      exactly what nobody remembered. Rolling a homefront move now asks, per roll - see
      module/dialogs/RollDialog.js, which every steading roll path opens.

      NOTE the `<section class="steading-moves-tab">` here has NO `.tab` class and sits
      outside `.sheet-body`. `.steading-moves-tab` carries `display: flex`, and an unlayered
      `display` on a `.tab` element beats core's layered `.tab { display: none }` - which
      would leak this panel onto every other page at once. Modern keeps the flex on an inner
      wrapper for the same reason. Do not merge the two shapes. --}}
<div class="stonetop-moves-sidebar steading-moves-sidebar{{#if stonetop.sidebarCollapsed}} is-collapsed{{/if}}">
	<button type="button" class="stonetop-sidebar-toggle"
	        aria-expanded="{{#if stonetop.sidebarCollapsed}}false{{else}}true{{/if}}"
	        aria-label="{{#if stonetop.sidebarCollapsed}}Expand moves sidebar{{else}}Collapse moves sidebar{{/if}}">
		<i class="fas fa-chevron-right"></i>
	</button>
	<div class="stonetop-sidebar-body">
	{{> "stonetop.section-heading" title=(localize "stonetop.steading.tabs.moves") collapse="steadingMoves"}}
	<section class="steading-moves-tab">

		<ol class="items-list steading-moves-list">
			{{#each stonetop.moves}}
			<li class="stonetop-move-item steading-move-row{{#unless rollable}}{{#unless interactive}} move-unowned{{/unless}}{{/unless}}">

				{{> "stonetop.steading-move-controls"}}
				<span class="stonetop-move-name stonetop-steading-move-open">{{label}}</span>
				{{#if description}}<div class="stonetop-basic-move-desc" hidden>{{{boldMissText description}}}</div>{{/if}}

			</li>
			{{/each}}
		</ol>

	</section>
	</div>
</div>
